{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793402012,"nonce":"KSkezppAXl8M6sMZHp4odGKGAFg=","message":"rayde-runner now talks to MSB only through the SandboxProvider interface (rayde_runner::Msb): storage (volumes.rs), toolchain restore (toolchain.rs), the session bridge (tunnel.rs) and the service's startup check, inventory, cleanup, session launch, project copy and boot (service.rs). command.rs keeps only the process and capture helpers the backend, git checkout and tests use. Verified on the NUC against real MSB 0.7.11 and a static Linux session binary: the runner's own end-to-end test (provisioning, reuse, isolation, restart, cleanup) passes on both the Alpine path (14 s) and the production toolchain path with the prepared toolchain snapshot (19 s); the backend's live conformance and allowlist tests still pass; all 15 unit tests pass; the NUC is left with no sandboxes or volumes. Found and fixed on the way: snapshot restores dropped --security restricted (the toolchain path passes it) and now keep it, with a named volume mounted by its disk file as the runner always did; the runner's disk checks (ext4, \u003cvolumes\u003e/\u003cname\u003e/disk.raw, a regular file, verified before removal) moved into the backend; a recovery boot fails when a volume is missing instead of creating empty ones. Unchanged behaviour to know about: boot now creates the sandbox and then copies the session binary in (it was --copy-file at create on the Alpine path), and each spawn and exec first checks the sandbox is running, which costs one status call. Service holds a concrete Msb; making it generic over the trait, so recovery flows can be tested against the in-memory fake without KVM, is the next step. Building the static session on the NUC needs these beside native/: rayscript, syntax, terminal, plus preview/ and tests/fixtures/images at the repo root (scratch copy in ~/rayde-conformance).","files":null}]}